WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
Error handling should help users, operators, and developers without exposing unnecessary detail.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.360
A user-facing error should be safe, clear, and limited.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:20.940
It should not reveal stack traces, connection strings, internal paths, tokens, raw exception details, sensitive records, or infrastructure information.

4
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.260
Diagnostic detail belongs in protected logs that are available to authorized operators.

5
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.400
Even there, logs need data classification.

6
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:39.260
Secrets, credentials, tokens, private keys, sensitive personal data, and excessive request content should be redacted or avoided.

7
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.320
Privacy-aware logging is part of application security.

8
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.400
Security visibility depends on logging the right events with useful context.

9
00:00:49.500 --> 00:01:02.940
Authentication events, authorization failures, suspicious validation failures, account recovery actions, administrative changes, configuration changes, high-risk data access, and deployment changes may all be relevant.

10
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.680
Logs should support investigation without becoming a second sensitive database.

11
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:20.040
Correlation IDs, request IDs, user or service identifiers, tenant identifiers, and event categories can make logs useful during operations and incident response.

12
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:29.580
The goal is to reconstruct what happened, when it happened, which component was involved, and what decision the application made without exposing more data than necessary.

13
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.900
Good logging also supports future review.

14
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.680
When teams can see recurring validation failures, permission denials, dependency errors, or deployment issues, they can improve the application.

15
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:45.100
Security visibility is not only for incidents; it is feedback for engineering.
